'''Mountain Park''' is a ghost town in western [[Alberta]], south of [[Cadomin, Alberta|Cadomin]], elevation 6200 feet,<ref>http://www.ghosttowns.com/canada/alberta/mountainpark.html Ghost Towns - Mountain Park</ref> at the end of the historic [[Coal Branch, Alberta|Alberta Coal Branch]] line of the [[Canadian National Railway]] (originally the [[Grand Trunk Pacific Railway]]).

[[Image:mountainpark.jpg|thumb|right|250px|The road into Mountain Park - an early colour photo]]

Production of [[steam coal]] for railroad use by the Mountain Park Coal Co. Ltd. began in 1912 or 1914.<ref name="Ghost Towns of Alberta">[http://www.ghosttowns.com/canada/alberta/mountainpark.html Ghost Towns of Alberta]</ref><ref>{{Cite web |url=http://www.coalking.ca/industry/historical_3.html |title=Alberta Online Encyclopedia: When Coal Was King |access-date=2007-03-10 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20070927062213/http://www.coalking.ca/industry/historical_3.html |archive-date=2007-09-27 |url-status=dead }}</ref> It closed in 1950 in response to rising debt, declining coal markets, and a flood by the [[McLeod River]] that washed out the railroad bed.<ref name=Kyba>{{cite book|last=Kyba|first=Daniel and Jane Ross|title=Exploring the historic Coal Branch|year=2001|publisher=Rocky Mountain Books, 336 p.|location=Calgary, Alberta|isbn=0-921102-83-6}}</ref>  Mining throughout the area ceased as the railroads replaced [[steam locomotive]]s with [[diesel locomotives|diesel]], and the town was quickly abandoned. Almost nothing remains of Mountain Park today, except for a restored cemetery and a few remnants of the mine. At its peak, the town was home to about 1,500 residents.<ref name="Ghost Towns of Alberta"/>

Mountain Park was the site of another coal mining operation, the Cheviot Mine, which opened in 2005 despite environmental opposition. Cheviot was operated by [[Teck Resources|Teck Coal Ltd.]] and produced [[coking coal]] for export to Japanese [[steel mills]].<ref name=Cheviot>{{Cite web|url=http://www.teck.com/Generic.aspx?PAGE=Teck%20Site/Diversified%20Mining%20Pages/Coal%20Pages/Cardinal%20River|title=Teck Cardinal River Operations|accessdate=2014-01-24|url-status=dead|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20140202164659/http://www.teck.com/Generic.aspx?PAGE=Teck%20Site%2FDiversified%20Mining%20Pages%2FCoal%20Pages%2FCardinal%20River|archivedate=2014-02-02}}</ref> It closed in 2020 due to declining coal reserves.<ref>{{Cite web|title=Cardinal River|url=https://www.teck.com/operations/canada/legacy/cardinal-river/|access-date=2021-06-13|website=Teck}}</ref>

